{{main|2014 Alabama gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Alabama|Governor]] [[Robert J. Bentley]] ran for re-election. Bentley was elected with 57.9% of the vote in 2010.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://elections.nytimes.com/2010/governor/alabama | title =Election 2010: Alabama Governor Profile | work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref>

Former [[Morgan County, Alabama|Morgan County]] commissioner Stacy Lee George challenged Bentley in the Republican primary, as did Bob Starkey, a retired software company executive.<ref>{{cite news|last=AP|title=Alabama's Bentley draws 2014 opposition from man who would be "gun-toting governor"|url=http://blog.al.com/wire/2013/04/alabamas_bentley_draws_2014_op.html|newspaper=AL.com|date=April 11, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://blog.al.com/wire/2014/02/bob_starkey_of_scottsboro_join.html|title=Bob Starkey of Scottsboro joins race for Republican nomination for Governor|work=AL.com|date=February 6, 2014|access-date=February 11, 2014|first=Mike|last=Cason}}</ref>

Former baseball player and businessman Kevin Bass and former [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] [[Parker Griffith]] pursued the Democratic nomination, which Griffith won.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://blog.al.com/wire/2013/12/business_owner_former_pro_base.html | title =Business owner, former pro baseball player Kevin Bass running for governor as a Democrat | author =Mike Cason | work=[[The Birmingham News]] | date =December 26, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://blog.al.com/breaking/2014/02/former_gop_congressman_parker.html|title=Former GOP Congressman Parker Griffith will run for Alabama Governor as a Democrat|work=AL.com|date=February 7, 2014|access-date=February 11, 2014|first=Steve|last=Doyle}}</ref>

Bentley won re-election to a second term.

