{{main|2014 Arizona gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Arizona|Governor]] [[Jan Brewer]] was term-limited in 2014 despite only serving one full term, as Arizona state law limits office holders to two consecutive terms, regardless of whether they are full or partial terms. In November 2012, Brewer declared she was looking into what she called "ambiguity" in Arizona's term-limit law to seek a second full four-year term.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2012/11/report-brewer-may-seek-3rd-term-083720?hp=f3 |title= Report: Jan Brewer may seek 3rd term as Arizona governor |work=[[Politico]] |date=November 12, 2012 |access-date=November 13, 2012}}</ref>

On March 12, 2014, Brewer announced she would not seek re-election to another four-year term, which would have required a "longshot court challenge" to the [[Arizona Constitution]].

[[Arizona Secretary of State]] [[Ken Bennett]],<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.eastvalleytribune.com/arizona/article_d1ea6446-218d-11e3-9909-001a4bcf887a.html | title =Ken Bennett says he'll run for governor | agency =[[Associated Press]] | publisher =eastvalleytribune.com | date =September 19, 2013}}</ref> [[Mayor of Mesa, Arizona|Mesa Mayor]] [[Scott Smith (Arizona politician)|Scott Smith]],<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.seattlepi.com/news/article/Mesa-Mayor-Smith-running-for-Arizona-governor-5128395.php |title=Mesa Mayor Smith running for Arizona governor |author=Bob Christie |agency=[[Associated Press]] |work=[[Seattle Post-Intelligencer]] |date=January 9, 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140116082125/http://www.seattlepi.com/news/article/Mesa-Mayor-Smith-running-for-Arizona-governor-5128395.php |archive-date=January 16, 2014 }}</ref> [[State Treasurer of Arizona]] [[Doug Ducey]],<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.azcentral.com/news/politics/articles/20130723ducey-files-paperwork-explore-arizona-governor-run.html | title =Ducey files paperwork to explore Arizona governor run | author =Yvonne Wingett Sanchez | work =[[Arizona Republic]] | date =July 23, 2013}}</ref> [[Arizona Senate|State Senator]] [[Al Melvin (politician)|Al Melvin]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://azstarnet.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/elections/state-sen-al-melvin-to-run-for-arizona-governor/article_13f5d53e-ab8f-11e2-b7bb-0019bb2963f4.html | title =State Sen. Al Melvin to run for Arizona governor | author =Hipolito Corella | work =[[Arizona Daily Star]] | date =April 22, 2013 }}</ref> former [[Go Daddy]] executive vice president Christine Jones,<ref>{{cite news | url=https://online.wsj.com/news/articles/SB10001424052702304868404579194241551597378 | title =Arizona Governor Race Gets More Crowded | author =Alejandro Lazo | work =[[The Wall Street Journal]] | date =November 12, 2013}}</ref> and former [[county attorney]] of [[Maricopa County, Arizona|Maricopa County]] [[Andrew Thomas (prosecutor)|Andrew Thomas]] sought the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.azcentral.com/news/politics/articles/20130425disbarred-former-maricopa-county-attorney-thomas-run-governor.html?nclick_check=1 | title =Disbarred former Maricopa County Attorney Thomas to run for governor | author =Alia Beard Rau | work=[[Arizona Republic]] | date =April 26, 2013}}</ref> Ducey won.

[[Fred DuVal]], former chairman of the [[Arizona Board of Regents]]<ref>{{cite web|last=AP|title=2014 Arizona governor race: Fred DuVal picks up endorsements from former governors|url=http://www.abc15.com/dpp/news/state/2014-arizona-governor-race-fred-duval-picks-up-endorsements-from-former-governors|publisher=ABC15|access-date=May 23, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131004221811/http://www.abc15.com/dpp/news/state/2014-arizona-governor-race-fred-duval-picks-up-endorsements-from-former-governors|archive-date=October 4, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> won the Democratic nomination.

Ducey won the election.

