[[Governor of Florida|Governor]] [[Rick Scott]] was elected with 48.9% of the vote in 2010, defeating then-Chief Financial Officer of Florida [[Alex Sink]] by a margin of just over 1 percent.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://elections.nbcnews.com/ns/politics/2010/florida/governor/ | title=Governor: Florida | work=[[NBC News]] | date=January 19, 2014 | access-date=January 20, 2014 | archive-date=February 1, 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140201192037/http://elections.nbcnews.com/ns/politics/2010/florida/governor/ | url-status=dead }}</ref> He announced his bid for a second term<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.ocala.com/article/20111004/WIRE/111009899 |title=Gov. Scott: I'll run again in 2014 |publisher=Ocala.com |access-date=November 29, 2012 |archive-date=September 24, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924122355/http://www.ocala.com/article/20111004/WIRE/111009899 |url-status=dead }}</ref> and faced former Republican governor turned Democrat [[Charlie Crist]]<ref>{{cite news|last=Izadi|first=Elahe|title=Republicans Scramble to Save Governor in Florida Battleground|url=http://www.nationaljournal.com/politics/republicans-scramble-to-save-governor-in-florida-battleground-20130523|newspaper=National Journal|date=May 23, 2013|access-date=May 23, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130607113752/http://www.nationaljournal.com/politics/republicans-scramble-to-save-governor-in-florida-battleground-20130523|archive-date=June 7, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> and Libertarian [[Adrian Wyllie]].<ref>{{cite news|last=March|first=William|title=Libertarian Wyllie declares for governor|url=http://www.tboblogs.com/index.php/news/comments/libertarian-wyllie-declares-for-governor|newspaper=Tampa Tribune|date=January 15, 2013|access-date=December 31, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140102110130/http://www.tboblogs.com/index.php/news/comments/libertarian-wyllie-declares-for-governor|archive-date=January 2, 2014|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

Democratic [[Florida State Senate|state senator]] [[Nan Rich]]<ref>{{cite news|last=Smith|first=Adam|title=Democrat Nan Rich says she's ready to take on Florida Gov. Rick Scott|url=https://www.tampabay.com/news/politics/national/while-bigger-names-ponder-2014-prospects-democrat-nan-rich-says-shes-ready/2122002/|newspaper=Tampa Bay Times|date=May 20, 2013}}</ref> lost to Charlie Crist in the primary.

Economist and 2010 Independent nominee for governor [[Farid Khavari]] also ran.<ref>{{cite web | url =http://www.sunshinestatenews.com/blog/farid-khavari-making-second-bid-governor-time-democrat | title =Farid Khavari Making Second Bid for Governor, this Time as Democrat | author =Kevin Derby | publisher =sunshinestatenews.com | date =October 29, 2013 | access-date =January 20, 2014 | archive-date =October 31, 2013 | archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20131031215150/http://www.sunshinestatenews.com/blog/farid-khavari-making-second-bid-governor-time-democrat | url-status =dead }}</ref>

Scott won re-election to a second term.

