{{main|2014 Hawaii gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Hawaii|Governor]] [[Neil Abercrombie]] launched his re-election campaign on April 29, 2013; sought a second term in 2014.<ref>{{cite web|url = http://www.hawaiinewsnow.com/story/22112805/abercrombie-launches-re-election-campaign-as-gop-opponents-mull-running-against-him |title=Abercrombie launches re-election campaign as GOP opponents mull running against him – Hawaii News Now |publisher=Hawaiinewsnow.com |access-date=April 29, 2013}}</ref> Abercrombie was elected with 58.2% of the vote in 2010 over former lieutenant governor [[Duke Aiona]]. However, in 2014, State Senator [[David Ige]] challenged Abercrombie for the Democratic nomination, and successfully defeated Abercrombie for the nomination in a landslide victory during the state's primary election on August 9, 2014. Abercrombie's primary election defeat was the first in Hawaii history for a governor, and marked the first time an incumbent governor lost re-election since [[William F. Quinn]]'s defeat in [[1962 Hawaii gubernatorial election|1962]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2013/07/09/hawaii-gov-abercrombie-gets-a-primary-challenger/ |title=Hawaii Gov. Abercrombie gets a primary challenger |date=July 9, 2013 |access-date=July 10, 2013 |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref>

In the midst of Abercrombie's loss, former lieutenant governor Duke Aiona won the Republican nomination for governor for the second time, and former [[Mayor of Honolulu|Honolulu mayor]] [[Mufi Hannemann]] won his primary as an independent. They along with David Ige advanced to the gubernatorial general election. Ige won the election.

