{{main|2014 Iowa gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Iowa|Governor]] [[Terry Branstad]] sought a sixth non-consecutive term.<ref>{{cite web|title=It's official: Terry Branstad will run for sixth term as Iowa governor|url=http://blogs.desmoinesregister.com/dmr/index.php/2014/01/15/its-official-terry-branstad-will-run-for-sixth-term-as-iowa-governor/article|access-date=January 20, 2014|archive-url=https://archive.today/20140118111140/http://blogs.desmoinesregister.com/dmr/index.php/2014/01/15/its-official-terry-branstad-will-run-for-sixth-term-as-iowa-governor/article|archive-date=January 18, 2014|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He was elected to a fifth term (non-consecutive) with 53% of the vote in 2010.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://elections.nytimes.com/2010/governor/iowa | title =Election 2010: Iowa Governor Profile | work=[[The New York Times]] | date =January 22, 2014}}</ref> Political activist [[Tom Hoefling]] unsuccessfully challenged Branstad for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web|title=Tom Hoefling announces 2014 Republican bid for Iowa Governor|url=http://www.tomhoefling.com/9/post/2013/12/tom-hoefling-announces-2014-republican-bid-for-iowa-governor.html|access-date=December 3, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131210021929/http://www.tomhoefling.com/9/post/2013/12/tom-hoefling-announces-2014-republican-bid-for-iowa-governor.html|archive-date=December 10, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

Assistant Majority Leader of the [[Iowa State Senate]] [[Jack Hatch]]<ref>{{cite web | url=http://blogs.desmoinesregister.com/dmr/index.php/2013/09/16/state-sen-jack-hatch-officially-enters-2014-race-for-iowa-governor/article | title=State Sen. Jack Hatch officially enters 2014 race for Iowa governor | author=Jason Noble | work=[[Des Moines Register]] | date=September 16, 2013 | url-status=dead | archive-url=https://archive.today/20130916171844/http://blogs.desmoinesregister.com/dmr/index.php/2013/09/16/state-sen-jack-hatch-officially-enters-2014-race-for-iowa-governor/article | archive-date=September 16, 2013 | df=mdy-all }}</ref> former [[Des Moines, Iowa|Des Moines]] school board member Jonathan Narcisse<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20140121/NEWS/140129876/1707 | title=Activist Jonathan Narcisse plans another bid for Iowa governor, this time as Democrat | agency=[[Associated Press]] | work=[[Omaha World-Herald]] | date=January 21, 2014 | access-date=January 23, 2014 | archive-date=February 3, 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140203223104/http://www.omaha.com/article/20140121/NEWS/140129876/1707 | url-status=dead }}</ref> and [[Webster, Iowa|Webster]] bus driver Paul Dahl,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://siouxcityjournal.com/news/local/th-democrat-enters-race-for-governor-sioux-city-stop-planned/article_6854614e-020b-5468-b557-dcff221f6e03.html|title=4th Democrat enters race for governor; Sioux City stop planned|date=October 2, 2013|access-date=October 14, 2013|work=[[Sioux City Journal]]|first=Bret|last=Hayworth}}</ref> sought the Democratic nomination. Hatch won.

Branstad won re-election and became the longest-serving governor in US history.

