{{main|2014 Massachusetts gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Massachusetts|Governor]] [[Deval Patrick]] was eligible to run for re-election, but decided not to seek a third term.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2013/12/21/his-time-office-winds-down-patrick-keeps-fund-raising-efforts/PGINv7QuZdpGWvqLO5QQcM/story.html | title=As his time in office winds down, Patrick keeps up fund-raising efforts | author=Matt Murphy | work=[[Boston Globe]] | date=December 21, 2013 | access-date=June 22, 2017 | archive-date=August 24, 2018 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180824125418/https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2013/12/21/his-time-office-winds-down-patrick-keeps-fund-raising-efforts/PGINv7QuZdpGWvqLO5QQcM/story.html | url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[Massachusetts Senate|State senator]] and [[Cape Air]] CEO [[Dan Wolf]] was running for the Democratic nomination, but withdrew after the Ethics Commission ruled his co-ownership of Cape Air violated state conflict of interest rules.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/news/politics/2013/08/22/state-senator-dan-wolf-resign-seat-suspend-gubernatorial-campaign/I3QUmeiChUtmFR6DB8m6LN/story.html|title=State senator Dan Wolf to resign seat, suspend gubernatorial campaign|date=August 22, 2013|access-date=August 22, 2013|work=[[The Boston Globe]]|first=Jim|last=O'Sullivan}}</ref>

Democratic candidates included [[PAREXEL]] executive [[Joseph Avellone]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.wbur.org/2013/07/12/avellone-mass-governor | title =Joseph Avellone Touts Private Sector Experience In Run For Governor | author =Bob Oakes | publisher=[[WBUR]] | date =July 12, 2013}}</ref> former Administrator of the [[Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services]] [[Donald Berwick]],<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/metro/2013/06/17/donald-berwick-former-obama-administration-official-kicks-off-bid-for-governor/BC7WaXd1eojBIgNSlMDX9L/story.html | title =Health leader Donald Berwick starts bid for governor | author =Joshua Miller | work =[[Boston Globe]] | date =June 18, 2013}}</ref> [[Massachusetts Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Martha Coakley]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://bostonherald.com/news_opinion/local_politics/2013/09/martha_coakley_to_run_for_governor_in_2014 | title =Martha Coakley to run for governor in 2014 | author =Matt Stout | work=[[Boston Herald]] | date =September 13, 2013}}</ref> [[Treasurer and Receiver-General of Massachusetts|Treasurer]] [[Steven Grossman (politician)|Steve Grossman]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.masslive.com/politics/index.ssf/2013/02/with_eye_on_2014_massachusetts.html | title =With eye on 2014, Massachusetts Treasurer Steven Grossman lays out 5 objectives he'd have as governor | author =Matt Murphy | publisher =masslive.com | date =February 27, 2013}}</ref> and former [[Department of Homeland Security|Assistant Secretary for Intergovernmental Affairs]] [[Juliette Kayyem]].<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.bostonmagazine.com/news/blog/2013/08/21/juliette-kayyem-jumps-in-for-guv/ | title=Juliette Kayyem Is Running for Governor | author=David S. Bernstein | work=[[Boston Magazine]] | date=August 21, 2013 | access-date=January 15, 2014 | archive-date=January 16, 2014 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140116111404/http://www.bostonmagazine.com/news/blog/2013/08/21/juliette-kayyem-jumps-in-for-guv/ | url-status=dead }}</ref> Coakley won the nomination.

Republican candidates included former Massachusetts cabinet official and 2010 nominee [[Charlie Baker]],<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.boston.com/uncategorized/noprimarytagmatch/2013/09/04/republican-charles-baker-releases-video-announcing-run-for-mass-governor/ | title =Republican Charles Baker releases video announcing run for Massachusetts governor | author =Michael Levenson | work=[[Boston Globe]] | date =September 13, 2013}}</ref> and TEA Party member and Shrewsbury small businessman Mark Fisher.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.masslive.com/politics/index.ssf/2013/12/westfield_native_mark_fisher_l.html/|title=Westfield native Mark Fisher launches Republican run for governor of Massachusetts|work=[[The Republican (Springfield, Massachusetts)|The Republican]]|first=Shira|last=Schoenberg|date=December 17, 2013|access-date=January 14, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140203004325/http://www.masslive.com/politics/index.ssf/2013/12/westfield_native_mark_fisher_l.html|archive-date=February 3, 2014|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Baker won the nomination.

Baker won the election.

