{{main|2014 Nebraska gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Nebraska|Governor]] [[Dave Heineman]] was term-limited in 2014.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2013/02/19/us/politics/mike-johanns-wont-seek-re-election-to-senate.html?_r=0 | title =Republican Senator From Nebraska Won't Run in 2014 | author =Jonathan Weisman | work =[[The New York Times]] | date =February 18, 2013}}</ref>

Former Republican [[Lieutenant Governor of Nebraska|lieutenant governor]] [[Rick Sheehy]] had been endorsed by Heineman, but Sheehy exited the race due to a report regarding a series of inappropriate phone calls he had made to women who were not his wife.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2013/02/02/nebraska-lt-gov-sheehy/1885801/ |title=Nebraska Lt. Gov. Sheehy resigns from office |work=[[USA Today]] |date=February 2, 2013}}</ref> [[Nebraska Legislature|State senators]] [[Tom Carlson]], [[Charlie Janssen]], and [[Beau McCoy]] also ran for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://journalstar.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/sen-tom-carlson-enters-governor-race/article_e6ec18c1-8c39-5ed4-9f0a-0e80d8a585ab.html|title=Sen. Tom Carlson enters governor race|date=July 12, 2013|access-date=July 12, 2013|work=Lincoln Journal Star|first=Don|last=Walton}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20130218/NEWS/130219525/1685#state-sen-charlie-janssen-to-run-for-governor-clare-won-t-run|title=State Sen. Charlie Janssen to run for governor; Clare won't run|date=February 18, 2013|access-date=February 18, 2013|work=[[Omaha World-Herald]]|first=David|last=Hendee|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130220235219/http://www.omaha.com/article/20130218/NEWS/130219525/1685#state-sen-charlie-janssen-to-run-for-governor-clare-won-t-run|archive-date=February 20, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>  Other potential Republican candidates include Auditor of Public Accounts [[Mike Foley (Nebraska politician)|Mike Foley]] and businessman [[Pete Ricketts]]. The nomination was won by Ricketts.

Executive director of the [[Center for Rural Affairs]] Chuck Hassebrook ran for the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://journalstar.com/news/local/hassebrook-will-enter-governor-s-race/article_3421fa27-589a-5950-af24-af68ed81f11a.html|title=Hassebrook will enter 2014 governor's race|date=June 2, 2013|access-date=June 2, 2013|work=[[Lincoln Journal Star]]|first=Don|last=Walton}}</ref> [[Nebraska Legislature|State Senator]] [[Annette Dubas]] was also running, but she has withdrawn, leaving Hassebrook the only Democratic candidate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20131125/NEWS/131129146/1685#dubas-withdraws-from-2014-nebraska-governor-s-race|title=Dubas withdraws from 2014 Nebraska governor's race|date=November 25, 2013|access-date=November 25, 2013|work=[[Omaha World-Herald]]|first=Martha|last=Stoddard|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131202222452/http://www.omaha.com/article/20131125/NEWS/131129146/1685#dubas-withdraws-from-2014-nebraska-governor-s-race|archive-date=December 2, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Hassebrook won the nomination.

Ricketts won the election.

