{{main|2014 Rhode Island gubernatorial election}}
[[Governor of Rhode Island|Governor]] [[Lincoln Chafee]] retired after one term in office.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.upi.com/Top_News/US/2013/09/04/RI-Gov-Lincoln-Chafee-announces-retirement/UPI-87561378321831/ | title =R.I. Gov. Lincoln Chafee announces retirement | work=[[United Press International]] | date =September 4, 2013}}</ref> Chafee was elected with 36.1% in a competitive three-way race in 2010 in which he ran as an independent.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://elections.nytimes.com/2010/governor/rhode-island | title =Election 2010: Rhode Island Governor Profile | author =Abby Goodnough | work=[[The New York Times]] }}</ref> He became a Democrat in May 2013, promoting speculation he would run for a second term, but later announced that he would not run for re-election on September 4, 2013.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2013/05/29/lincoln-chafee-governor-rhode-island-democrat/2369503/ | title =R.I. Gov. Chafee to become Democrat, run for 2nd term | author =Catalina Camia | work=[[USA Today]] | date =May 29, 2013}}</ref>

[[Providence, Rhode Island|Providence]] Mayor [[Angel Taveras]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.providencejournal.com/breaking-news/content/20131029-providence-mayor-angel-taveras-launches-campaign-for-r.i.-governor.ece | title =Providence Mayor Angel Taveras launches campaign for R.I. governor | author =Randal Edgar | work=[[Providence Journal]] | date =October 29, 2013}}</ref> State Treasurer [[Gina Raimondo]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wpri.com/news/politics/state-politics/gina-raimondo-announces-she-will-run-for-ri-governor-in-2014 |title=Gina Raimondo announces she'll run for governor: Treasurer may face Taveras, Pell in Dem primary |author=Ted Nesi |publisher=wpri.com |date=December 18, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131222140526/http://www.wpri.com/news/politics/state-politics/gina-raimondo-announces-she-will-run-for-ri-governor-in-2014 |archive-date=December 22, 2013 }}</ref> and former [[United States Department of Education]] official [[Clay Pell]] ran for the nomination.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://ripr.org/post/clay-pell-talks-hes-made-his-mind-run-governor | title =Clay Pell Talks Like He's Made His Mind Up To Run for Governor | author =Ian Donnis | publisher=[[Rhode Island Public Radio]] | date =January 16, 2014}}</ref> Raimondo won the primary election.

[[Cranston, Rhode Island|Cranston]] Mayor [[Allan Fung]] ran for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite news|last=McGowan |first=Dan |title=Doherty: No plans to run for governor |url=http://www.wpri.com/news/local/mcgowan/doherty-no-plans-to-run-for-governor |newspaper=WPRI |date=May 17, 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131016082125/http://www.wpri.com/news/local/mcgowan/doherty-no-plans-to-run-for-governor |archive-date=October 16, 2013 }}</ref> [[Moderate Party of Rhode Island|Moderate Party]] Chairman [[Ken Block (politician)|Ken Block]], who received 6.5% of the vote in the 2010 gubernatorial election, had filed to run again for the Moderate Party.<ref>{{cite news|last=Nesi|first=Ted|title=Analysis: Field of RI governor candidates coming into focus|url=http://blogs.wpri.com/2013/05/21/analysis-field-of-ri-governor-candidates-coming-into-focus/|newspaper=WPRI|date=May 21, 2013|access-date=June 2, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130615123941/http://blogs.wpri.com/2013/05/21/analysis-field-of-ri-governor-candidates-coming-into-focus/|archive-date=June 15, 2013|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref> He has since switched to run as a Republican. Fung won the nomination.

