[[Governor of Texas|Governor]] [[Rick Perry]] was eligible to run for re-election, but chose not to seek a fourth term on July 8, 2013.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-us-canada-23235693 |title=Texas Governor Rick Perry to retire|work=BBC News |date= July 8, 2013|access-date=July 8, 2013}}</ref> Perry was re-elected to a third term with 55.1% of the vote in 2010.

Attorney General [[Greg Abbott]] was the Republican Party nominee,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/texas-gop-gubernatorial-candidate-doesn-support-insulting-tweet-wendy-davis-article-1.1430882 |title=Greg Abbott, Republican Attorney General running for Texas Governor |author=Leslie Larson |work=[[New York Daily News]] |date=August 19, 2013 }}</ref> having defeated perennial candidate [[Larry Kilgore]],<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/24/us/politics/with-stickers-a-petition-and-even-a-middle-name-secession-fever-hits-texas.html | title =With Stickers, a Petition and Even a Middle Name, Secession Fever Hits Texas | author =Manny Fernandez | work =[[The New York Times]] | date =November 23, 2013}}</ref> [[Lisa Fritsch]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lisa4texas.com/|title=Lisa Fritsch for Governor|access-date=November 10, 2014}}</ref> and former [[Univision]] personality Miriam Martinez in the Republican primary.<ref>{{cite web | url =http://www.themonitor.com/news/local/article_31f26b70-6449-11e2-95a9-001a4bcf6878.html | title =Miriam Martinez says she'll run for governor | author =Dave Hendricks | work =[[The Monitor (Texas)|The Monitor]] | date =January 22, 2013 | access-date =January 15, 2014 | archive-date =November 16, 2018 | archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20181116181419/https://www.themonitor.com/news/local/article_31f26b70-6449-11e2-95a9-001a4bcf6878.html | url-status =dead }}</ref>

