{{main|2014 Washington, D.C., mayoral election}}

[[Mayor of the District of Columbia|Mayor]] [[Vincent C. Gray]] sought re-election. Gray was elected with 74.2% of the vote in 2010.

Gray faced a competitive primary with challenges from four members of the [[Council of the District of Columbia|district council]], including [[Muriel Bowser]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nbcwashington.com/news/local/Bowser-Announces-Mayoral-Run--199680071.html|title=Bowser Announces Mayoral Run|date=March 24, 2013|access-date=April 13, 2013|work=[[WRC-TV|NBC4 Washington]]}}</ref> [[Jack Evans (Washington, D.C., politician)|Jack Evans]],<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nbcwashington.com/blogs/first-read-dmv/Sherwoods-Notebook-The-Race-Is-On-190064721.html|title=Sherwood's Notebook: The Race Is On|date=February 6, 2013|access-date=March 18, 2013|work=[[WRC-TV|NBC4 Washington]]|first=Tom|last=Sherwood}}</ref> [[Vincent Orange]],<ref>{{cite news |title= Vincent Orange is running for mayor |newspaper= The Washington Post |date= November 8, 2013 |first= Mike |last= DeBonis |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/mike-debonis/wp/2013/11/08/vincent-orange-is-running-for-d-c-mayor/ }}</ref> and [[Tommy Wells]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/dc-politics/wells-kicks-off-mayoral-campaign/2013/05/18/f42d3190-bf05-11e2-97d4-a479289a31f9_story.html|title=Wells kicks off mayoral campaign|date=May 18, 2013|access-date=May 22, 2013|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|first1=Mike|last1=DeBonis|first2=Tim|last2=Craig}}</ref> as well as former [[United States Department of State|State Department]] official [[Reta Jo Lewis]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/groups_urge_end_to_dc_riders_on_bills_district_notebook-226117-1.html?pos=hbtxt|title=Groups Urge End to D.C. Riders on Bills|date=July 5, 2013|access-date=July 8, 2013|work=[[Roll Call]]|first=Jason|last=Dick}}</ref> and activist [[Andy Shallal]].<ref name="DeBonis">{{cite news |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/mike-debonis/wp/2013/11/08/andy-shallal-is-running-for-d-c-mayor/ |title= Andy Shallal is running for D.C. mayor |first= Mike |last= Debonis |newspaper= The Washington Post |date= November 8, 2013 }}</ref> Bowser defeated Gray for the Democratic nomination by over 10 points.<ref>{{cite news |work= District of Columbia Board of Elections |title= Primary Election |date= April 23, 2014 |url= https://www.dcboee.org/election_info/election_results/2014/April-1-Primary-Election |access-date= April 17, 2014 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140412074919/http://www.dcboee.org/election_info/election_results/2014/April-1-Primary-Election |archive-date= April 12, 2014 |url-status= dead |df= mdy-all }}</ref>

[[David Catania]], another district councilman, and [[Carol Schwartz]], a former councilwoman and perennial candidate, ran in the general election as independents.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://washingtonian.com/2014/03/11/david-catania-will-run-for-dc-mayor/|work=Washingtonian|date=March 11, 2014|access-date=March 11, 2014|title=David Catania Will Run for DC Mayor|first=Benjamin|last=Freed}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Carol Schwartz, former D.C. Council member, launches independent mayoral campaign |first=Mike |last=Debonis |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=June 9, 2014 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/dc-politics/carol-schwartz-former-dc-council-member-launches-independent-mayoral-campaign/2014/06/09/558bcb6e-f005-11e3-bf76-447a5df6411f_story.html }}</ref> Other candidates included Libertarian nominee Bruce Majors and [[D.C. Statehood Green Party|Statehood Green]] nominee [[Faith Dane]].<ref>{{cite news |title= Gay Libertarian enters mayor's race |work= Washington Blade |date= November 27, 2013 |first= Lou |last= Chibbaro Jr. |url= http://www.washingtonblade.com/2013/11/27/gay-libertarian-enters-mayors-race-bruce-majors/ }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Race for D.C. mayor now a little less crowded |work= Washington City Paper |first= Mike |last= DeBonis |date= January 2, 2014 |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/dc-politics/race-for-dc-mayor-now-a-little-less-crowded/2014/01/02/a30c7980-73f8-11e3-9389-09ef9944065e_story.html }}</ref>

Bowser won the election, becoming the second female mayor of the District of Columbia since [[Sharon Pratt]] left office in 1995. She was also elected to the lowest share of the vote in Washington, D.C. history.

